<article class="prose">
  <h2 id="log">The change log</h2>
  <p>A collection is a list of changes. Each change sets one field of one record:</p>
<pre><code>{`{ "t": "1789520000000-0001-k7f3a2c9d1e4x0z9", "kind": "accession", "id": "rmu3e054w01k7f3a2c9d1e4x0z9", "field": "notes", "value": "sulked all summer" }`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>t</code> is a hybrid logical clock: 13-digit millisecond wall time, a hex counter of four digits (up to six, only when a millisecond holds more than 65,535 changes), and the writer: the 12-character id of the device that made the change followed by a 4-character tag for the browser tab (each tab of one device is its own clock), 16 characters in all; a change written by the app's own number repair carries a writer starting <code>zz</code> instead. It orders by wall time, then counter value, then writer. To find one device's changes, match the first 12 characters of the writer against the backup manifest's <code>device</code>, not the whole. A device follows a peer's clock only up to five minutes ahead of its own, so one phone set to the wrong year cannot become every device's clock. <code>kind</code> is one of <code>accession</code>, <code>event</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>sowing</code> (what the app calls a propagation batch; a <code>location</code> is a place), <code>photo</code>, <code>taxon</code>, <code>setting</code>. A delete is a change setting <code>_deleted</code> to true; a later change to any other field of that record revives it. The fields <code>id</code>, <code>kind</code> and <code>_t</code> belong to the record and cannot be set by a change (nor can the names <code>*</code> and <code>_deleted=</code>).</p>
  <p>To read a collection, sort the changes by <code>t</code> and apply them in order, keeping for each record and field the value with the greatest <code>t</code>. The result is the same whatever order the changes arrived in, and applying a change twice changes nothing. This is the whole merge rule; backup restore, the v2 import and sync all use it.</p>
  <p>Record shapes are plain JSON with flat fields. Records are keyed by an opaque identity (<code>id</code>, minted once on the device that made the record and never shown: a kind prefix, <code>r</code> for a plant, <code>s</code> for a batch, <code>l</code> for a place, <code>p</code> for a photograph, <code>e</code> for an event, then the minting stamp's wall time as exactly 8 base-36 digits (the app reads the time as the 8 characters after the prefix), its counter in two or more base-36 digits, and the writer, so an id looks like <code>rmu3e054w01k7f3a2c9d1e4x0z9</code>; ids minted before round eight carry a 12-character writer, and ids from the first builds are short strings such as <code>r1</code>; all remain valid); the number a plant is known by is a field, <code>acc</code>, unique among live and dead plants and never reused, so two devices that mint the same number offline end up with two plants, one of them renumbered and told so, never one plant made of both. Records from before this separation used the number as the id; readers take <code>acc</code> when present and the id otherwise. Sowings carry their batch number in <code>no</code> the same way. <b>Joins are by id, never by number:</b> an event's <code>acc</code> and a photo's <code>acc</code> hold the plant's record id (the <code>r1</code>, not the <code>2026-0001</code>), and for a sowing's own events (<code>germinate</code>, <code>potup</code>, <code>loss</code>) the sowing's record id; a photo of a batch carries the sowing's id in <code>sowing</code> instead. To list a plant's events, take the plant's <code>id</code> and select the events whose <code>acc</code> equals it.</p>
  <p>The fields, by kind. <b>accession</b>: <code>acc</code>, <code>taxonName</code>, <code>taxonKey</code> (GBIF), <code>nameAsReceived</code>, <code>cultivar</code>, <code>nameKind</code> (species, cultivar or hybrid), <code>parentage</code>, <code>fieldNumber</code>, <code>provenance</code> (wild, f1, fn, veg, unknown), <code>status</code> (growing, archived, dead), <code>locationId</code> (and a legacy <code>location</code> name from before places were records), <code>acquired</code> (YYYY-MM-DD), <code>sourceFrom</code>, <code>sourceRef</code>, <code>sourceForm</code>, <code>price</code>, <code>notes</code>, <code>sowingId</code>, <code>cover</code> (a photo id), <code>importedOn</code> (the day the record arrived here by import). <b>event</b>: <code>acc</code> (a record id, as above), <code>d</code> (YYYY-MM-DD), <code>t</code> (acquire, repot, water, feed, treat, prune, flower, pollinate, seed, graft, measure, move, note, death, audit, germinate, potup, loss), and as the type needs <code>note</code>, <code>cause</code>, <code>used</code>, <code>followUp</code> (days), <code>measures</code> (an object of named numbers), <code>n</code> (a count: seedlings up so far, plants potted, plants lost). <b>photo</b>: <code>acc</code> or <code>sowing</code>, <code>d</code>, <code>dFrom</code> (exif or added), <code>caption</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>bytes</code>, <code>sha</code> (SHA-256 of the full-size JPEG, hex); its pixels are stored separately under the same id. <b>location</b>: <code>name</code>, <code>parentId</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>indoor</code>, <code>floorC</code>, <code>ppfd</code>, <code>lightHours</code>, <code>lat</code>, <code>lon</code>, <code>altM</code>, <code>notes</code>, <code>sort</code>; temperatures are stored in °C whatever the display. <b>sowing</b>: <code>no</code>, <code>taxonName</code>, <code>taxonKey</code>, <code>cultivar</code>, <code>nameKind</code>, <code>parentage</code>, <code>method</code>, <code>parentAcc</code> (a plant's record id), <code>sown</code> (YYYY-MM-DD), <code>count</code>, <code>sourceFrom</code>, <code>sourceRef</code>, <code>provenance</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>container</code>, <code>treatment</code>, <code>bottomHeatC</code>, <code>covered</code>, <code>locationId</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>notes</code>, <code>importedOn</code>. <b>taxon</b> (keyed by the species slug): <code>name</code>, <code>gbifKey</code>, <code>myNotes</code>, <code>removed</code>, <code>followed</code>. <b>setting</b>: one record, id <code>numbering</code>, with <code>scheme</code>, the numbering scheme every device shares.</p>

  <h2 id="backup">The backup file</h2>
  <p><code>cultifolio-YYYY-MM-DD.cultifolio.zip</code>, an ordinary zip:</p>
<pre><code>{`manifest.json        { format: "cultifolio-backup", v: 1, exported: <ISO time>, device, app,
                       counts: { changes, accessions, events, locations, sowings, taxa, photos, photoBytes },
                       photosMissing: [ids of photo records whose pixels were not on the exporting device; absent when none],
                       scheme }   (photos counts the photographs actually in the file)
changes.json         a JSON array of changes, the whole log, as above
photos/<id>.jpg      full-size JPEG, long edge 1600 px
photos/<id>.t.jpg    320 px thumbnail
plants.csv           one row per plant, for a spreadsheet (not for import)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Restoring merges by the log rule, so restoring an old file over a newer collection loses nothing and restoring twice changes nothing. The older changes-only JSON export (<code>{`{ "format": "cultifolio-changes", "changes": [...] }`}</code>) is still accepted, as is a v2 Herbarium backup.</p>

  <h2 id="sync">Sync on the wire</h2>
  <p>One vault key per person: 30 symbols from an alphabet without I, L, O, U, 0 or 1, in six groups of five. From it, HKDF-SHA-256 with salt <code>cultifolio-vault-v1</code> derives three 32-byte keys; the input keying material is the 30 symbols upper-cased with every character outside A–Z and 0–9 removed (dashes, spaces, a line break, and the <code>cultifolio://vault?k=</code> prefix of the pairing link are all dropped, which is what the app does to whatever is typed or scanned): the UTF-8 bytes of that 30-character string, not a decoding of it: an AES-256-GCM key (info <code>enc</code>), a 256-bit auth token (info <code>auth</code>, sent as 64 hex digits in a bearer header) and an HMAC-SHA-256 key for naming batches (info <code>name</code>). The vault id is 26 symbols made from SHA-256 of the string <code>id:</code> + the token's 64 hex digits: take the first 26 bytes of the digest and map each byte <i>b</i> to <code>ALPHABET[b mod 30]</code>, where the alphabet is <code>ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ23456789</code> (the same 30 symbols the key uses, in that order). It is not a base-32 encoding of the digest. The server stores SHA-256 of <code>token:</code> + token and compares; at creation, the one moment it sees the token itself, it also checks that the id is the one that token derives, so a vault cannot be made under a name its key would not produce.</p>
  <p>A sealed blob is one version byte (1), a 12-byte IV, then AES-GCM ciphertext (a 128-bit tag, appended as WebCrypto does) with associated data the UTF-8 bytes of <code>vaultId|kind</code>, where kind is <code>log</code> or <code>photo</code>, so a blob cannot be replayed into another vault or as another kind; a photo's data also carries its id (<code>vaultId|photo|photoId</code>), so it cannot be handed out under another photo's id; photographs sealed by builds before that binding carry <code>vaultId|photo</code> alone, and a reader should try the named form first and the plain form if the tag fails, as the app does. A log batch decrypts to <code>{`{ "v": 1, "device": "...", "changes": [...] }`}</code> and is stored at <code>vault/&lt;id&gt;/log/&lt;hour&gt;-0000-&lt;device&gt;-&lt;fingerprint&gt;.bin</code> where <code>&lt;hour&gt;</code> is the hour (in ms, padded to 13 digits) of the batch's last change, <code>&lt;device&gt;</code> the pushing device's 12-character id and <code>&lt;fingerprint&gt;</code> the first twelve hex digits of HMAC-SHA-256, under the vault's naming key, of exactly the bytes JavaScript's <code>JSON.stringify(changes)</code> gives: no spaces, keys in the order each change carries them; a serialiser that adds spaces or sorts keys gives a different fingerprint. Every seal uses a fresh IV, so the same batch pushed twice is different bytes under the same name; the pushing device sends the full fingerprint (<code>X-Batch-Plain</code>) and its id (<code>X-Device</code>), and when both match what the server kept with the first copy it answers that the batch is already there and stores nothing. Different bytes under a held name without that match are refused (409), so a name never stands for two contents. The fingerprint is keyed so that the server holds nothing it could test a guessed plaintext against; earlier builds named batches by the last change's full HLC and an unkeyed SHA-256, and those names remain valid. A batch the device cannot open or validate is set aside by name and reported, and does not stop the ones behind it; a batch that cannot be stored on the device (a full phone) is not set aside: the sync stops with an error and fetches it again next time. A change stamped more than five minutes ahead of the receiving device's clock is stored but held out of the merge until that clock reaches it, and the sync page says how many are waiting; otherwise one fast phone would win every field it touched on every device. Batches are handed out in the order they <em>arrived</em> at the server, not in key order: a device that edited offline and uploads after another device has already pulled past that time must still be found, so a client asks for everything since a server arrival time, re-reads a minute of overlap, and skips batches it already holds; within one sync it pages on the pair (arrival, key), so many batches arriving in the same second cannot repeat. The changes inside carry the HLCs that settle merges; arrival order only decides discovery. A photo decrypts to a 4-byte big-endian length, the full JPEG, then the thumbnail, at <code>vault/&lt;vaultId&gt;/photo/&lt;photoId&gt;.bin</code>. Nothing is rewritten or deleted.</p>
  <p>The endpoints: <code>POST /api/sync/vault</code> {`{ id, token, create }`}; <code>GET /api/sync/log?vault=&amp;since=&lt;ms&gt;</code> (returns <code>{`{ batches: [{ key, at }], more, next?: { at, key } }`}</code>; the next page is <code>&amp;after=&lt;at&gt;:&lt;key&gt;</code>); <code>POST /api/sync/log?vault=</code> with headers <code>X-Batch</code>, <code>X-Batch-Plain</code>, <code>X-Device</code>; <code>GET /api/sync/log/&lt;hour&gt;-0000-&lt;device&gt;-&lt;fingerprint&gt;?vault=</code> (the batch name, as above, without the <code>.bin</code>); <code>PUT|GET|HEAD /api/sync/photo/&lt;id&gt;?vault=</code>. All but creation take <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. A body larger than the limit is 413; a vault with no room left (2 GB) is 507 with <code>{`{ error: "vault full", bytes, limit }`}</code>, and the device stops sending and says so rather than splitting the batch. Every refusal is plain JSON with an <code>error</code> the device can show. One address (an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 /64) is bounded: 5 new vaults and 3 GB stored per day, 600 requests per ten minutes to open, list and push and 3,000 to fetch or store single batches and photographs; past any of these the answer is 429 with <code>Retry-After</code> in seconds, and the device simply tries again later. New vaults are also bounded for everyone, per day and in all; past either the answer to a creation is 503 with a sentence saying so, joining an existing vault is never refused by it, and the sync page shows the sentence as it is. The byte counts live in KV, kept per object; a listing of the vault puts them right on every open and once a day, so two uploads in the same instant can overshoot the allowance by at most one object until then.</p>
  <p>What the server can see. It holds a vault id, a hash of the token, and sealed blobs, and it cannot open any of them. But the blob names, sizes and arrival times are in the clear: a batch is named by the hour and device id of its last change, a photograph by the time and device id of its creation, and a sealed photograph is its full JPEG plus its 320-pixel thumbnail plus 17 bytes (a 4-byte length, the version byte and the IV) and the 16-byte tag. So the operator can count the devices in a vault, see to the hour when each one made changes and to the second when it synced, count the batches (roughly, how often changes are made) and the photographs, and read their sizes. This is not zero-knowledge in the strict sense: the pattern of activity is visible, its content is not. Nothing about the plants themselves (names, notes, places, dates, the number of plants) is visible, and nothing is ever written into a vault by anyone but its devices.</p>
  <p id="privacy">The reference lookups your own pages make are the other channel. A plant page, the labels, the plants list and the front page's own tiles need the reference's figures for your species, and they ask for them by hash bucket, never by name or key: the species slug is hashed (FNV-1a) into one of 32 buckets of roughly 280 species, the device asks <code>/api/sheets?b=</code> or <code>/api/entries?b=</code> for the buckets it needs and picks its own species out of the answer. The server sees which buckets a device asked for, which narrows each species to one in about 280, and no more; a species page you open is, of course, a page you opened. The worker keeps the answers for the corpus (each request carries the corpus id from <code>/api/corpus</code>), so a second visit asks nothing until the corpus is refreshed. The reference's photographs are not fetched for your own pages unless you switch them on in Settings; then they come from the image host directly, which sees which species this address asked a picture of.</p>

  <h2 id="dossier">A species dossier</h2>
  <p>Each species page is rendered from one JSON document, served at <code>/api/dossier/&lt;gbifKey&gt;</code>: name and classification, native range as TDWG level-3 codes, open occurrence points with licence tags, the map marker and how it was placed, the climate envelope (median year, 10th and 90th percentile years, the cells and records it rests on, the typical cell) with its sources, extremes, photographs with author and licence, the summary with its licence, literature, links, and an <code>upstream</code> block recording for every source whether it answered, refused, or was carried from an earlier build. The corpus is built from public data under CC0, CC BY and CC BY-SA; the repository's <code>npm run export</code> packages a built corpus as a dataset bundle (the species JSON, <code>species.csv</code>, <code>climate.csv</code>, and the licence terms per source), which is how the reference data leaves the site as data rather than pages.</p>
</article>

